assuming that nuisance1, nuisance2, and condition were of class factor, R
will given you something along the lines of a simple effects model for
model4. I am saying "something along the lines of", because I'm not sure
whether R will automatically use the right factor coding. In any case, if
the predictors were all factors, you probably got the same number of dfs
for model 1 and 4, but instead of the main effect and interaction, you now
got simple effects. For example, model4 should contain estimates of the
effect of nuisance1==baseline for both condition=A and condition=B,
something like:

nuisance1:condition==A
nuisance1:condition==B

and so on for nuisance2, etc.

Is that what happens? The first test you provide above is what you want
(model 3 vs. 1). That's testing the omnibus effect of condition and any of
its interactions. In combination with omnibus tests of the effect of the
different higher order interactions that condition has, this will provide
the information you need. (i.e. only the three-way removed, the three-way +
two-way with nuisance1 removed, the three-way + two-way with nuisance2
removed, the three-way + two-ways with nuisance1 and 2 removed, everything
removed). that's pretty much parallel to the ANOVA logic.

hth?

Florian

